              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the last word of the chapter: what did kevalam mean, and what did muhuḥ mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: अहो",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "अहो (aho) — \"oh!\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "अहो (aho) — \"oh!\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is the sound of being struck by something: delight, surprise or dismay, with the tone deciding which. अहो (aho) on its own is a whole reply, and one of the few you can give without knowing what comes next."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is two small cries stuck together rather than a word with parts, which is why there is no history to trace under it. Sanskrit wrote its exclamations down, and this one has been in the language for as long as anything in it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five more replies: then, as, only, again and again, and oh."
            }
          ]
        },
